WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.480
A SOC 2 Type I report evaluates control design and suitability at a specific point in

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:06.420
time.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.960
The central question is whether the organization has described the system and controls

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:15.500
appropriately and whether those controls are suitably designed to meet the applicable

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:20.920
Trust Services Criteria as of the report date. It is a snapshot, not a movie.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:24.100
Type I can be useful early in a SOC 2 program.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.640
A first-time organization may need to show customers that it has defined the system,

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.660
selected appropriate criteria, mapped controls, assigned owners,

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.040
and put key control designs in place.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:40.660
A Type I report can help validate that the control environment is ready to operate and can

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:45.600
support customer assurance while the organization prepares for a later Type II review

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:49.260
period. The focus is design. For example,

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:54.040
an access review control may state that user access to in-scope systems is reviewed

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.580
quarterly by responsible managers.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.280
A Type I audit may look at whether that control is defined, assigned,

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.740
aligned to the criteria, and supported by a process that could reasonably work.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.320
It may inspect evidence that the control exists at the report date,

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.380
but it does not show that quarterly reviews happened consistently for months.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.280
That limitation matters.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.580
A Type I report does not prove that controls operated over a long period.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.320
It does not demonstrate that change management was followed repeatedly,

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:28.180
that monitoring alerts were reviewed over time, that vendors were reviewed on schedule,

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.280
or that exceptions were consistently remediated.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.300
It can show readiness, but not sustained operation.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.680
Organizations sometimes overuse Type I language in sales conversations.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.340
It is better to be precise.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.760
Type I can provide point-in-time assurance about control design.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:48.620
It may be appropriate when a program is new, when customers want early evidence,

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.600
or when the organization needs a structured milestone before Type II.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:58.100
But customers with mature vendor risk programs may eventually expect Type II because they

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.000
want assurance that controls operate, not just that they are designed.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.340
For teams preparing for Type I, the work is still real.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:09.400
The system description must be accurate. Controls need owners.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:14.260
Policies need to match actual processes. Evidence should be honest and traceable.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.240
Exceptions should be documented. Type I is not the finish line;

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:22.700
it is often the first disciplined checkpoint on the path to sustained readiness.
